# Settings

## Introduction

The core's modifiers and helpers read their rules from a settings object. To call them directly, every field below has to be present:

```ts
const settings = {
	defaultCoordinates,
	defaultVisibleArea,
	areaPositionRestrictions,
	areaSizeRestrictions,
	sizeRestrictions,
	positionRestrictions,
	aspectRatio
};
```

The five restriction fields form `CoreSettings`. The two defaults belong to `InitializeSettings` and are only read when a state is created. `InitializeSettings` also has two optional fields, `defaultTransforms` (the starting rotation and flips) and `priority` (described under [`createState`](/docs/concept/modifiers#createstate)).

Almost every field accepts either a value or a function of `(state, settings)` that returns the value. Functions are called each time the field is needed, so they can depend on the current state, for example on the image size.

<Admonition type="tip" title="In Svelte">

You don't build this object yourself when you use `Cropper`. Pass the fields you care about as props, such as `sizeRestrictions` or `defaultCoordinates`, and the cropper fills in the rest with [`createDefaultSettings`](/docs/concept/defaults). If a setting function reads `$state`, the cropper re-checks its state when that value changes. Read the final settings with `cropper.getSettings()`.

</Admonition>

## Fields

### Default coordinates

`defaultCoordinates` decides where the crop starts.

```ts
type DefaultCoordinates<Settings = CoreSettings> =
	| CoordinatesTransform
	| CoordinatesTransform[]
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: Settings) => CoordinatesTransform | CoordinatesTransform[]);

type CoordinatesTransform =
	| Partial<Coordinates>
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: CoreSettings) => Partial<Coordinates> | null)
	| null;
```

It can be a coordinates object, a function returning one, or an array of either. The value is applied with the same logic as the [`setCoordinates`](/docs/concept/modifiers#setcoordinates) modifier, so an array is applied step by step: for example a size first, then a function that centres the result.

### Default visible area

`defaultVisibleArea` decides which part of the image is shown at first.

```ts
type DefaultVisibleArea<Settings = CoreSettings> =
	| VisibleArea
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: Settings) => VisibleArea);
```

It is either a visible area object or a function that returns one. The result is still fitted to the boundary's aspect ratio and the area restrictions.

### Area position restrictions

`areaPositionRestrictions` limits where the **visible area** can be, in image pixels.

```ts
type AreaPositionRestrictionsSetting =
	| AreaPositionRestrictions
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: CoreSettings) => AreaPositionRestrictions);

interface AreaPositionRestrictions {
	top?: number;
	left?: number;
	right?: number;
	bottom?: number;
}
```

Each field is the outermost allowed edge. Leave a side out to leave it unrestricted, and pass `{}` for no restriction at all.

### Area size restrictions

`areaSizeRestrictions` limits how small or large the **visible area** can be, which bounds how far the image can be zoomed.

```ts
type AreaSizeRestrictionsSetting =
	| AreaSizeRestrictions
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: CoreSettings) => AreaSizeRestrictions);

interface AreaSizeRestrictions {
	minWidth: number;
	maxWidth: number;
	minHeight: number;
	maxHeight: number;
}
```

For no restriction, use:

```ts
{ minWidth: 0, minHeight: 0, maxWidth: Infinity, maxHeight: Infinity }
```

When both maximums are finite, the core adjusts one of them so they match the boundary's aspect ratio.

### Position restrictions

`positionRestrictions` limits where the **crop** can be, in image pixels.

```ts
type PositionRestrictionsSetting =
	| PositionRestrictions
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: CoreSettings) => PositionRestrictions);

interface PositionRestrictions {
	top?: number;
	left?: number;
	right?: number;
	bottom?: number;
}
```

Pass `{}` to let the crop go anywhere, including outside the image.

### Size restrictions

`sizeRestrictions` limits how small or large the **crop** can be, in image pixels.

```ts
type SizeRestrictionsSetting =
	| SizeRestrictions
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: CoreSettings) => SizeRestrictions);

interface SizeRestrictions {
	minWidth: number;
	maxWidth: number;
	minHeight: number;
	maxHeight: number;
}
```

For no restriction, use:

```ts
{ minWidth: 0, minHeight: 0, maxWidth: Infinity, maxHeight: Infinity }
```

Missing fields are treated as `0` for minimums and `Infinity` for maximums. The maximums are also capped so the crop always fits inside the position restrictions.

### Aspect ratio

`aspectRatio` limits the crop's width-to-height ratio.

```ts
type AspectRatioSetting =
	| AspectRatio
	| ((state: CropperState, settings: CoreSettings) => RawAspectRatio);

interface AspectRatio {
	minimum: number;
	maximum: number;
}

type RawAspectRatio = Partial<AspectRatio> | number;
```

A function may return a single number for a fixed ratio, or an object with only one bound. For no restriction, use `{ minimum: 0, maximum: Infinity }` (a function can simply return `{}`).
